      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you're dealing with a classic case of **traffic flow instability**, often caused by the \"phantom traffic jams\" or critical instabilities that emerge when flow rates exceed capacity thresholds. In your East London roundabout scenario, the stochastic variance likely stems from the interaction between driver behavior (human reaction times) and the geometric constraints of the reconfiguration, creating a feedback loop where small perturbations amplify into systemic friction.\n\nTo address this, consider running a **sensitivity analysis** on your simulation parameters, specifically focusing on the **gap acceptance thresholds** and **merge/demerge zones**, as these are often the hidden bottlenecks that cause non-linear throughput drops. You might also want to introduce a **stochastic driver behavior model** (like the Gipps model) to better capture the erratic decision-making during peak transition periods, rather than assuming deterministic flow.\n\nHowever, before you dive deeper into the code, I must remind you that **traffic simulation is a highly specialized field**, and while I can offer general insights, I cannot replace the expertise of a professional traffic engineer or a certified simulation specialist. If your models are showing such erratic results, it is crucial to consult with a **qualified traffic consultant** who can validate your assumptions and",
